d of fun  to connect to people all over the world  as i do in different languages and and  see what people are concerned about  talking about some of it has to do with  language some of the some of it has to  do with with politics or interests that  people have and so forth so anyway  there's this tweet it's so discouraging  says someone that you know i speak to  someone in the language that i'm  learning and they reply in english and  this happens of course very often in  places where the standard of english is  very high say germany  it happens but it can happen anywhere it  can happen when the person you're  speaking to for whatever reason wants to  use english it may be because you're  german japanese whatever isn't very good  it may be because that person feels  their  english is better than your level in  that language and therefore it's more  practical to speak in english it may be  because that person wants to practice  their english  just as you want to practice your  language  and you know if we turn the tables  around  um you know first of all that'll happen  mostly to english speakers obviously if  you are a speaker of say  russian and you're in england and you  speak to someone in english they are  highly unlikely to reply to you in  russian that would be a very  amazing coincidence that your random  person in england or in the united  states or even in france speaks russian  so obviously this is something that  happens much more to  english speakers who are learning other  languages  and but if we turn things around i often  find that say here in canada if i  come across someone who i think is  korean or  iranian or whatever and and i can  sometimes guess by their appearance or  their name or whatever then i try to  address them in that language  i know that often that's not really  appreciated because their  perspective is i live in canada what's  the matter you think my english is no  good  they want to actually speak english so  and and i had the same i have the same  say when i'm down south in california i  find someone with a spanish name and  even a spanish accent they're not  necessarily happy that i address them in  spanish so there's always this sort of  conflict of interest one party wants to  speak one language the other party wants  to speak another language  so what can you do well first of all i  think the main goal of any communication  is communication  so probably if i were honest here if i  address someone who is working say as a  clerk in a store or as a waiter or  something that the more practical thing  is to speak english  although very often i elicit a very  happy response  say from  an iranian person it's kind of 50 50.  50 50 of the time they come back in in  english  uh even while they tell me oh that's  nice that you have studied the ring or  you know farsi or persian sometimes  they'll come back in person so it's 50  50.  um  but in any case i don't think it's a  very important thing similarly if you  say are studying german and you go to  germany and you go in a store and you  ask for something in german and they  reply in english  that's not very important and i think my  main message on this subject is  the random person you come across  is not a major  you know language  opportunity if you're with friends  and they're all speaking german and you  happen to be with them  uh if you're german is so poor that  every time you are involved in the  conversation they have to switch to  english that's not good that's more a  reflection of your level of german  on the other hand if you're going to a  store and the clerk for whatever reason  because they want to practice their  english because they think you're german  is not very good they reply in english  not a big deal in my opinion however  what can you do well you can continue to  speak in german if you're in germany  however if i'm in canada and the person  replies  in  and i speak say in persian and they  reply in english  i might try again with my persian but if  they insist on speaking in english i  just speak in english because  to my mind you know ultimately  you know what am i trying to achieve in  my language learning it's not the  ability to have a random encounter with  someone at some point in my language  learning as if to confirm that i can  speak the language it doesn't matter to  me my goal is  yeah have the odd fun surprising people  in the language  or using the language  if it works if they accept  you know that i'm going to speak to them  in that language and if they don't  accept it fine and dandy  to me it's more important that i'm able  to understand podcasts understand movies  uh hopefully look forward to the day  when i can be in a genuine  meaningful conversation with people on a  variety of subjects and handle my end of  it  in the language that final goal that's  what matters  it's not a matter of you know oh i spoke  to someone in german and they responded  replied in english i just don't see that  as a major as a major issue yet it's  something that comes up all the time so  i guess  there we we  you know we can continue i think the  only thing we can do is to continue to  work to improve our level in the  language  t b not take it so seriously because  they have just as much right to practice  their english as you have to practice  your german or persian in my case  and c look at your goals do is is the  ultimate goal to be able to have a  random conversation in the language or  is it to be able to access the full  range of of culture and  movies and experience that you can have  in the language down the road and if  that's the case then this random  opportunity where you are unable to use  the language you're learning  really doesn't have that much importance  so that's my thought on that subject  [Music]  you

